so what i now wanted to do was just  touch briefly on  exome sequencing in the prenatal setting  rather than the postnatal as  i've just been talking about so it's a  bit of a background we know that fetal  ultrasound detects anomalies in about  two to five percent of pregnancies  and these abnormalities can range from  isolated minor abnormalities to those  that may involve multiple systems and be  much more severe  another roughly 4 000 monogenic diseases  we know at least 200 of them have a  prenatal manifestation  and that list is constantly growing as  our understanding improves and  and our learning in this area increases  um but what we do know already is that  genetic disorders are a leading cause of  morbidity and premature death in the  prenatal and neonatal period  so when a case has an underlying genetic  diagnosis  it's really really important that  they're identified to allow for that  accurate management  and diagnosis is clearly also important  in terms of enabling reproductive  decision making  and also assessment of recurrence risk  for patients  so as i'm showing here current testing  strategies are only about  able to achieve a genetic diagnosis in  around 40 percent of cases  but we know that the real figure is  likely to be much higher than that so  there will be a subset of that 60  undiagnosed who do have a genetic  abnormality it's just that we can't  detect  it using the methods that we we have in  place at present  and when i think about you know current  testing strategies what i'm  considering here is qfpcr perhaps a  g-bounded carrier type and of course  microarray and there will no doubt be  other standard testing protocols that  are in place  so the yield for exome sequencing  clearly varies  uh in both the pre and the postnatal  setting depending on the  patient selection and when we think  about the prenatal setting the the  pregnancy cohort that we're looking at  and  so some studies however have suggested a  yield of up to 80  which i'm showing on the slide here but  so others have found it to be much lower  but  what i now wanted to do is just focus on  um  the exome service that we provide here  at congenica  and in particular i wanted to highlight  for you some complex cases that we've  used uh  exome sequencing to resolve  so we've been running this exome service  uh for around two years and we've had  analyzed over 600 cases and those are  both postnatal and prenatal  um this has all been done in conjunction  with  uh st george's hospital in south london  amongst other  um amongst other customers for whatever  better word  um and i wanted to particularly focus on  georgia's hospital here however because  the cases that i'll show later on in  this presentation were all referred by  them  um but as i'm showing here on the slide  so  the uh the pipeline that we use  incorporates a copy number testing  element  and it's really fantastic to be able to  offer this single comprehensive test  we've achieved this in conjunction with  our colleagues at nonicos in developing  the  xocg assay which allows for this  assessment of exome depth  on the slide here i've illustrated the  steps that i would consider to be owned  by congenica if you like and when i say  congenica i refer both to the platform  itself  and the underlying bioinformatic  pipeline but also the clinical  interpretation that myself and my  colleagues within our clinical and  scientific team  that we perform a few key features to  highlight would be as i've said the copy  number aspect by  xmcg and exome depth but also the  variant prioritization capabilities by  examizer  as well as comprehensive reference data  in the form of decipher  clinvar we also have mastermind  integrated within the product which is a  fantastic variant level literature  searching tool  um and if we think about so  what kind of results are we getting well  you can see that the vast majority of  those  634 cases are postnatal  but we do as i've said offer a rapid  service for prenatal cases  and our diagnostic yield across the  across the um patient groups across the  postnatal prenatal setting is  sits at 23 in terms of a varan  interpretation approach i don't have  time to talk about that extensively  today but i just wanted to uh to touch  on it quickly is um  we do use acmg guidelines and we  consider  standard lines of evidence when  performing our interpretations such as  functional studies and segregation  information  however what i really wanted to to point  out today is that we have a  we have a really unique set up with some  georges and that we have a  an extremely collaborative and close  working relationship with the physicians  and the whole care team  and as such we have a real depth of  phenotypic information  at our disposal and i think this has  benefited us  hugely and allowed for the upgrading or  downgrading  of variants of uncertain significance in  many many cases  so we we certainly feel it's truly worth  if possible investing in a mostly  a truly multidisciplinary approach um  just to say we do we do report variants  of uncertain significance and clearly  they can represent um  for one of a better word a burden in in  some ways so wherever possible it's good  to be able to review those and resolve  them um  either either way um i won't be talking  directly any more about the congenica  platform and its capabilities  but um i some of the cases do show some  aspects of what a powerful tool it is  in helping us to solve and these complex  and often challenging cases  so what i'm showing here is a breakdown  of the yield for postnatal cases which  stands at  21 on a previous slide i showed that  some studies have seen a yield of about  40  however it's important to note that the  yields as i've said will vary depending  upon the cohort selected  and we don't keep our referrals as such  so we see a wide range of phenotypes not  just  neurodevelopmental disorders however the  pie chart to the right  is showing that the vast majority of our  referrals um  are in patients with developmental  disorders and to the left you'll see  hopefully it's  clear enough on the sides to the left  you will see um  the diagnostic yield in this phenotypic  group so in the neurodevelopmental  disorders  compared to other phenotypic groups is  the highest  um both figures also give you an idea of  the breadth of referrals we see so we  see everything from  cardiac referrals to endocrine and  absolutely everything in between so um  and just a final note to say that we do  see mostly trios um so we do see um  but we will take uh other family  structures so singletons and  situations where you have affected  siblings we handle them  all of those so what i now wanted to do  was just to move to discussing an  interesting postnatal case with you  so this was a a patient a 13 year old  girl referred to us for investigation of  developmental delay  and an epilepsy phenotype she also has a  similarly affected sibling  um a huge amount of testing had been  undertaken  prior to this exome including enrollment  in multiple large-scale sequencing  studies such as the  100 000 genomes project here in the uk  but none of this uh none of this testing  had managed to find a cause for this  particular child's condition  so using the uh congenica platform  we were able to detect one maternally  inherited missense variant but  arguably more interestingly one  paternally  inherited synonymous variant that is  predicted to  create a cryptic splice site in the  alg-11 gene  so the phenotype of alg-11 now which is  a um congenital disorder of  glycosylation the phenotype is a good  fit for both of the affected siblings  so um there are we believe two reasons  why this finding was not detected using  any of the other  sequencing approaches that had been  performed well  firstly the biochemical screening and so  the normal biochemical screening result  that the patient had had doesn't  fit with the finding of alg 11. hence we  feel that it's possible that this  particular disorder or the possibility  of this disorder  was not flagged up for the clinician or  care team  and this really highlights the challenge  clinicians are faced  faced with around genotype phenotype  relationships and the fit of them  as i touched on in the introduction to  this and  secondly the synonymous variant was  filtered out by other bioinformatics  pipelines  um the congenica splicing annotation  capabilities which are very powerful  plus  the ability for us to really easily see  segregation data which  showed the variant was shared between  the affected siblings and that it was  paternally inherited  really brought this variant to our  attention  so if we think about what the outcome  here was well follow-up testing if the  patient  uh showed shortened alg um transcripts  um although splicing in this particular  gene is actually quite difficult to  interpret  and digging a little bit deeper into the  literature showed that normal  biochemical screening is actually often  seen in alg 11. so that fits with the um  normal iso um iso electric uh focusing  of transferrin seen in both siblings  that i mentioned earlier  um further testing is now in progress to  look for reduction of protein levels um  however the hbo lc and the thin layer  chromatography has already shown  truncated sugar structures in both of  the girls  so the finding was of great relief to  the family and we really do hope to  soon be definitively able to end the  diagnostic odyssey for them once that  follow-up testing is complete  so if we now focus our attention to the  prenatal work that we've been doing  so as i said the majority of our  referrals um are postnatal but in that  prenatal group the majority of our  referrals have actually been  from pregnancies with multiple  congenital anomalies  um with skeletal dysplasias giving the  highest yield of any phenotypic group  and that figure sits at around 69  as you can see in the graph um to the  left um  so really high um and we accept all  family structures as i said um  however the majority of our cases are  trios um  trio testing as just to touch on a  little bit further  we know that it's not always feasible or  appropriate for some families however  having parental information is really  useful in terms of the efficiency gains  to be had when looking at the mode of  inheritance for a variant  and clearly when you don't have uh  parental samples you can't determine if  a variance denovo  and this can really be an important  piece of evidence when assigning  pathogenicity  and we do find that in many cases this  this information about the de novo  status is is the difference between a  variant of uncertain significance and a  pathogenic assessment  uh i wanted to highlight the point as  well that as with post-natal  reports we do um we do report variants  of uncertain significance and  some may argue that the variant of  uncertain significance burden here is  even higher in some ways  so good to be able to remove that where  at all possible  so um of the 120 cases that we've tested  we're getting an overall yield of about  31  and this includes two cases where we've  identified a cnv of clinical  significance  so one of those copy number variants um  it's going fantastic in terms of  efficiency so thinking about time and  money saving to be able to detect  cnvs and the smvs and indels in tandem  so on to my second case study and this  is a prenatal referral  so this was a referral from an unrelated  couple who had one previous healthy  child but also a history of miscarriages  and by the 20-week scan uh short femurs  were present and a suspected fracture  although  calcification of the skull was actually  normal um  by 33 weeks however boeing of the right  female was present and there was  evidence of  further fractures so there was clearly  um a query at this stage of whether this  was an osteogenesis imperfecta  so what we did is we undertook a  singleton xn sequencing in this instance  focusing on the  osteogenesis imperfecta gene panel from  panlap which is a  genomics england curation resource um if  any of you haven't accessed panel app  before  i would really urge you to go and take a  look at it after after today and as it's  a really fantastic resource and  we do rely on one that rely on it we use  it quite heavily and along with our own  kind of assessment manual gene curation  effort but yeah really fantastic  resource  so what we found is two variants in the  alpl gene one missense and one splicing  and the splicing variants predicted to  impact the nearby donor site  this particular splicing variant has  been described in multiple affected  patients with one homozygous case being  lethal  the missense variance missense variant  had been described in the compound  head state in multiple patients but this  as i said this was the singleton case so  but without parental samples it was  really hard for us to call these  variants anything other than of  uncertain significance  now um alpl is associated with  uh defective mineralization of bone and  teeth um in the  presence in the presence of low activity  of serum and bone alkaline phosphatase  and both dominant and recessive modes of  inheritance have been described  so what we did is we followed this  testing up with parental studies to give  us both segregation data but also  looking at the biochemical result  of these in the parents testing is  unreliable in pregnant women but the  father was found to have a low alp  which is clearly consistent with the  findings as carriers would also be  protected to have low alp  and this finding actually gave a much  better prognosis than osteogenesis  imperfecta and the pregnancy continued  with the right care in place to support  the family  if we think about postnatal management  so postnatal metabolic assessment  was performed and confirmed a diagnosis  of infantile hypophosphatasium  